    .{ .slug = "css-word-wrap", .title = "word-wrap", .category = "CSS", .search = "word-wrap css-word-wrap word-wrap, word-break, overflow-wrap CSS", .description = "The word-wrap CSS property (now standardized as overflow-wrap) controls whether the browser should break words to prevent them from overflowing their container. When set to break-word, it forces otherwise unbreakable strings to wrap onto the next line to avoid layout issues.", .notes = "", .tested = "2025-05-08", .summary = .{ "Supported", "Partial (#1)", "No (#2) on Windows; No on Windows Mail, Android; Supported (#3) on Outlook.com, iOS", "Supported" }, .clients = "Apple Mail\nmacOS: 16: Supported.\niOS: 16: Supported.\nGmail\nDesktop Webmail: 2025-05: Partial (#1).\nAndroid: 2025-05: Partial (#1).\n#1: Applies word-wrap: break-word to all items.\nOutlook\nWindows: 2016–2021: No (#2).\nWindows Mail: 2025-05: No.\nOutlook.com: 2022-05: Supported (#3).\niOS: 2025-05: Supported (#3).\nAndroid: 2025-05: No.\n#2: Does not honor any wrapping, including word-wrap, overflow-wrap, and word-break. Using an equivalent MSO breakdown (-ms-word-break: break-all and mso-hyphenate: none together) does not work.\n#3: Supports both word-wrap and equivalent MSO breakdown (-ms-word-break: break-all and mso-hyphenate: none together)\nYahoo! Mail\nDesktop Webmail: 2025-05: Supported.\nProtonMail\nDesktop Webmail: 2025-05: Supported.\niOS: 2024-11: Partial (#4).\nAndroid: 2024-11: Partial (#4).\n#4: word-wrap is correctly supported, however word-break seems to break for all values" },
